---
scope: src/tools/inspect
status: aligned
description: Code introspection tool that returns a named symbol's documentation, signature, and kind without opening the full source file.
intent: >
  aide_inspect gives agents Tier 2 progressive disclosure for code — the
  ability to read a symbol's documentation, signature, and kind without
  opening the file or loading the function body. When an agent knows a
  symbol name from an import list or orchestrator file, it calls inspect
  to learn what that symbol does and what shape it takes, all from
  structured metadata rather than raw source. This lets agents decide
  which helpers to drill into at Tier 3 and which they can use based on
  the contract alone.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - Given a symbol name, the tool returns the shape and documentation an agent needs to use that symbol on contract alone — no body, no full file.
    - The tool searches the host project's workspace source, not the AIDE server's own source.
    - An agent that calls inspect and gets a result back has enough information to decide whether to read the full file or to use the symbol based on its contract alone.
    - When the named symbol is not found, the tool returns a clear not-found result rather than an error or empty response that leaves the agent guessing.
  undesired:
    - Returning the function body along with the signature. The point of Tier 2 is contract without implementation; including the body burns context the agent did not ask for.
    - Silently returning a partial match or a different symbol when the exact name is not found. A wrong match is worse than no match — the agent will act on a contract that does not belong to the symbol it asked about.
    - Requiring the agent to specify a file path to use the tool. The tool should search across workspace source so agents can inspect symbols they know by name but have not yet located.
    - Failing on common source-language symbol forms — arrow functions assigned to const, exported type aliases, class methods, and the like. A tool that only handles plain function declarations misses most real-codebase symbols.
---
